{
  "image_name": "0ecf5e2698028dca0e8e17019ad31808.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how autophagy-related membranes interact with viruses and viral proteins at several stages of infection. It shows viral particles or components being captured in membrane compartments, then either targeted for degradation, used for immune processing, or in some cases exploited by viruses.\n\nA central theme is selective autophagy, or virophagy, in which damaged endosomes or viral cargo are recognized by factors such as galectin 8, p62, ubiquitin, and other host proteins, leading to enclosure within an autophagosome and fusion with degradative compartments. Several examples from different viruses are placed around this pathway, indicating that host antiviral factors can restrict infection by routing viral material into autophagic destruction.\n\nAt the same time, the diagram highlights that some viral proteins counteract or hijack these pathways. Different viruses appear to manipulate autophagosome formation, membrane trafficking, or cargo selection for their own replication or persistence.\n\nAnother major outcome is antigen handling. Viral material delivered to endolysosomes or the proteasome/ER pathway is processed for MHC class II or class I loading, including cross-presentation, and then displayed to CD4+ and CD8+ T cells. A side panel suggests donor-cell exocytosis can transfer virus-containing vesicles to another cell, extending this membrane-based trafficking route.",
